A white limousine parked in a poverty-stricken neighborhood of Louisiana. A man in a white suit stepped out of said limo, his face beaming a godray of white light. It was blinding to look at, and beautiful. He closed the limo door and began to walk with such a stride that it appeared he was gliding through the air, gracefully. He checked his watch, smiling. Nobody who saw him knew how they knew he was smiling, but they knew he was smiling. He disappeared in a flash of light.



Down the street from the limo -- well, where it //was//, because now it's here, down the street, somehow -- the man in the white suit stepped out of an alleyway, his outfit lighting up the darkened street. To his right, a poor beggar man lied on a cardboard box. He walked to the beggar and kneeled down, looking at the man.



"You are without a home?" he asked the man.



"Yes. I was evicted last week and ain't got no place to go," he responded.



"I can help with that." The one in the white suit snapped his fingers and with a small flicker, the beggar man had a wallet and stacks of cash that could make even the richest blush.



"Oh God, thank you so much!" the no-longer-poor exclaimed. "How could I ever repay you?"



"There's no need. Take care." The man turned around to be met with his conveniently parked limousine, which he would get into and disappear around a corner. The newly rich man on the sidewalk ran to borrow a phone.

