The man in white sat in a warehouse he reserved for certain occasions, such as the one he would be attending to shortly. He checked his watch every half-minute, waiting for nobody.



He had him



Nobody stepped out from the shadows, having been concealed the entire time. But he figured the one in the chair already knew that.



"Caught you," the man in white chuckled out.



"Fuck you talkin' about? I caught you," Nobody clapped back.



"Yeah, I was joking around. How long have you been following me?"



Nobody stared at him, his face covered in shadow. "Following? Only a few weeks. That's more than you need to know." Nobody stepped forward. "What's your name?"



"Yannick. Do I have the privilege of knowing yours?"



"Call me Pluto."



Nobody snapped his fingers and a chair was summoned opposite of Yannick. He sat in it and studied Yannick's body language. He was tense but steady, his hands twitching and his right leg kicking up and down. His face, lit up but readable, was as collected as could be in the court of Nobody's observation, showing drips of nervousness in an ocean of collectiveness.



Nobody began. "Do you know The Man in the White Suit?"



"Well of course I know him. He's me!" Yannick responded. Nobody scoffed.



"The Star Wars quote? Really? You've gotta be kidding," Nobody replied. You could never tell from his voice or unmoving body, but he was angry. "The original. Randomly appeared globally and performed random acts varying from petty crime to exposing corrupt politicians. Ring any bells?"



Yannick shrugged.



Nobody sat in thought for a second. //How much does this guy not know?// "Does 'Foundation' mean anything to you?"



"Nope," Yannick blurted out. Nobody kept his gaze fixed on the man.



"Yannick, you have to stop your cliché miracle shit okay? There's a group of people out there who target those like you and they will make your life hell. Turn back. You've already been recorded en masse so they could be on your tail as we speak. Stop."



Yannick laughed. "What are you, a conspiracy theorist? I'm helping people. I'm sure your shadow government would understand that--"



"They don't see grey. Only black and white. When I tell you they will ruin you, I mean it. You have to disappear, Yannick."



Yannick thought for a second. "Why do you even care? Just a minute ago, you were on my ass about some other white suit, now you're concerned for my safety? Just calm it down. I'm using my gift to help people where I can and here you come with the 'group of people who target those like me' bullshit. Maybe you're just jealous of what I can do."



"Oh shut up, Yannick. You don't even scratch the surface of what I can do. And no, that's not what this is." Nobody sighed. "I've been studying you for some odd weeks because I thought you were someone from my collective past that I had assumed had been dead for a decade. Now that I've dispelled that entirely, I want to help." He continued, "You're a good, albeit very typical person helping where he can but there are people out there who will track you and lock you up and never let you see any light that isn't coming from your head again. It was a mistake to appear in public so much, but when not even knowing about them?" Nobody laughed to himself. "It's a damn miracle you aren't already in a cell. You need to leave, for good."



Yannick stared at him. "Fine," he muttered. He was angry, but got the memo.



"Good man. Take that white limo of yours and everything you hold dear and go live at some nice resort away from people. Oh, and I guess you won't have to be using any of the things in this warehouse to fight me," he quipped.



"What? How did you know that?"



"Nobody knows everything. Bye-bye." Yannick blinked and nobody was there. He sat in the darkness for a moment before flicking the lights on with a snap.



"Pluto, huh?"

