[[div class="blockquote"]]

**STEP ███/███:** 

Enter site from south; cf. coordinates C024.

[[/div]]

[[<]]

Ghosts roamed the halls.



    Or, at least, that’s what they looked like.

    

    When Mark looked side to side, he saw just that; specters, with eyes dark and shaded, without an inch of skin showing, masks oozing mist as they stalked the corridors. He grasped his rifle tight as those shadows crept on, beams of light emanating from their weapons and methodically sweeping the walls. And he crept on with them.

    

    A voice, clouded in static, cut through the hissing silence. “Third on the right.”



    Counting the corridors — one, two, three — they turned. Despite their outward air, they had dreadful little knowledge on the facilities they raided; and it didn’t help that so many of these drab, white corridors looked the same. Sometimes text above their heads indicated where they were, but the letters and numbers meant nothing to anyone. They were just gibberish. Hateful. Everyone looked down when they came up, rifles pointing straight ahead through the next door, the next turn, the next hall… Mark didn’t, though. It was just code. And code couldn’t mess with your head, not on its own.

    

    In case anything went wrong, he felt better keeping an eye on it. Memorizing it. The way in, the way back. The landmarks, if you could call them that — the distinctive turns, the double-backs, the doors… whether they opened them or not. Soldiers of the Insurgency would loudly complain that Foundation facilities were frustrating mazes, and that without the Sergeant’s guidance, they’d never find their way around. They’re just strange, the soldiers whispered. They do everything wrong. Unnaturally. The minds of the Insurgency knew the way forward. They had the plan, and all its mighty steps. In the end, they really didn’t need to trust in anything more than that.

    

But… still. Mark didn’t stop counting corridors and memorizing landmarks. Foundation facilities weren’t mazes, in the same way that their code wasn’t gibberish. The Foundation just wrote, spoke, and built in a language that he didn’t understand. And just because you don’t understand something doesn’t mean you should close your eyes when it’s around.



He kept an eye out. Remembered. Just in case anything went wrong. Although, to be fair, nothing ever had gone wrong so far. As long as they followed everything they were told, exactly and perfectly.



    They had the plan, after all. Step-by-step, moment by moment.

    

    “Fifth,” the voice came again, rising among them like fog. “Right side.”

    

They counted the doors as they walked. One, two, three, four, five. They stopped.



When they did, all their beams of light turned outwards from the door. They shot through the dark and stayed still. Among those lights, three figures made their way through the ranks and reached for the door. Mark didn’t see exactly what they were doing; but after a few moments, they heard a soft chime, an electric whirring, and a sharp intake of breath.



    “Shane, Shane, Dammon, and Dault,” came the voice again. “With me.” The Sergeant walked up and put a hand on the door.

    

    On hearing his last name twice, Mark readied himself. He gripped the rifle tighter and looked around until he found the eyes of another of the ghosts. The figure met his eyes too, and nodded.

    

    The door opened. 

    

    A light was on in the room, and for a moment it was blinding; the specters lost their luster and the mist they breathed was briefly burned away. The Sergeant strode into the white void and Mark followed, with three others close by. The door closed.

    

    Mark ducked into position, scanning the room. 

    

Lab. Tables. One, two, three men — only one being armed.



    Before he’d even had the chance to get used to the light, another one of his companions shot a volley of silenced bullets that missed the armed guard by at least a foot. The guard cursed loudly and ducked, raising his rifle.

    

Keeping low, Mark rushed to a table for cover — but the guard didn’t fire back. No, instead he yelled something unintelligible to the two other men in the room and rushed to the side, to a lever embedded into the wall.



    Mark lined up his shot, waited, and… cursed as bullets whizzed past his arm, just barely missing him. 

    

They’d come from behind. He shot a glance back. One of his companions met his gaze briefly. He seemed shocked. Or perhaps apologetic? Hard to tell from the other side of the mask.



    Shots sped across the room. Every time the Insurgency took a shot, it was silenced, shadowed and difficult to tell from whom it came — but they all missed. When the one guard took his shots, they were obvious, visible, and he also missed. But they came closer. And his shots weren’t silenced. They bathed the room in thunder.

    

Bullets collided with the walls with sharp, stinging sounds. Another burst of shots from one of Mark’s detachment missed completely, spraying a table with holes and sending files flying onto the floor. The Sergeant yelled something, but outside of the darkness and the quietude of the corridors his voice lost its usual timbre and no-one seemed to hear him.



    A mess, Mark thought. A fucking mess. All of them.

    

Someone else shot, and Mark heard a thud.



But the guard was still standing, and so was every man in his detachment. One of the unarmed men — a scientist — in the room was on the floor, though. Clutching the wrong side of his chest.



Cursing, Mark swung from behind his table and raised his weapon. He waited. He pulled the trigger. The guard’s blood splattered onto the fallen files and he collapsed, his head cracking against the floor. 

[[/<]]

