[[div class="blockquote"]]

**STEP ███/███:** 

Sub-priority: ‘Charlie.’

[[/div]]

[[<]]

The firefight had been terribly loud, in a fairly quiet location. During a presumably stealthy mission.

 

    But was that a problem? Apparently not. Because they’d done exactly what they’d been asked. The orders hadn’t been to be silent — the orders had been to make sure to come moments before night fell, to make sure the door was closed, and to make sure no one rang any alarms. And they followed those to the letter. One might infer from those orders that stealth was paramount — but apparently such an inference was not, itself, part of the plan. So the Sergeant walked into a room stained with the blood of two men and projected pure serenity. As though he’d predicted all of this all along. 

    

    Mark pursed his lips behind his mask and stood back up from behind his cover, smoothing out his camo with the back of a gloved hand. The Sergeant walked up to the two scientists — one still dying from a shot to the liver, the other struggling against the grip of one of their soldiers — and started whispering something. 

    

Without too long he gestured to the door and another soldier opened it, allowing two more figures inside. Two men in sleek white overcoats, which in the darkness outside had stood out like a sore thumb but now blended right in with the bloodied lab. They pulled their gas masks off with a sharp hiss, and without a look at the soldiers around them they strode forwards. Mark watched them with eyes narrowed behind his own mask, clutching his rifle.



They stood over the two crouching scientists, softly speaking to them in a tone so nonchalant it was almost funny when put in the context of a man bleeding out on the floor.



    They spoke too quietly for anyone to understand anything, but eventually, the dying man spat.

    

    “You tell me,” he said loudly. “What happened… You tell me.”

    

    After a few moments, the two men both took a step back, overcoats billowing in unison. They looked less like men, and more like… splotches of white, just figures matching the walls and the blinding lights above them. Where they stood, the tables and boards behind them looked like they vanished. The Sergeant grabbed his weapon and, ignoring the widening eyes of his prisoners, promptly ended the interrogation.

    

    “God,” breathed a voice next to Mark.

    

    Mark turned. Another soldier stood there, shorter than he was, more spindly. In the navy, he would’ve looked comically out of place. But here? Although in the dark they stalked as one, Mark’s new brethren were the most mismatched, misshapen group of killers he’d ever seen. This one’s gear was too large for him, and he’d had to roll up his sleeves slightly so he could even hold his rifle.

    

    Mark looked him up and down. “Oliver Dault, right?”

    

    Behind his mask, Dault nodded. 

    

    Mark returned the nod. “Do me a favor. Aim before you fucking shoot.”

    

    “Right. Sorry.”

    

    After the two corpses flopped to the floor, the white figures, which Mark referred to only by the names One and Two, began their routine inspection of the lab’s contents. That was going to take a little while.

    

    Next to him, Dault had begun observing the bullet holes in the walls. “Huh.”

    

    Mark looked at them — huh was right. Where Mark had expected a small hole, some cracks and drywall falling into little piles of white dust, he instead saw… gashes. Metal. These walls were metal.

    

    “Thin,” Dault said. “They’re very thin. Corten steel. Maritime-grade.”

    

    Mark glanced at Dault. “Where’s Philip?”

    

    Dault nodded to the other end of the room.

    

    Mark thanked him curtly and walked off, adopting the military gait that he could barely ever shake. The other soldiers walked about, trying — in vain — to stop themselves from looking over the papers strewn about the lab, or glancing at the posters and guides on the walls. It turns out, having the fanaticism of the perfect soldier doesn’t also grant you the effects of rigorous military training.

     

    Philip was holding his gun in one hand and using it to flip through pages on a clipboard.  When he saw Mark approach, he smiled through his mask. Most people wouldn’t have been able to tell, but Mark had learned to recognize the creases in the fabric. 

    

“You know,” Philip said quietly, “I don’t think they can actually read this stuff either. I think everyone’s just pretending.”



    “Doubt it.”

    

    Philip shook his head. “I mean, look at this. Nonsense. Imagine how much more they’d get done if it was just plain English.”

    

    Mark didn’t answer, pursing his lips. 

    

    Philip glanced at him. “Nice shot, by the way.”

    

    Mark nodded slowly. Then, he turned his head towards his brother. “Was it you who did the other one?”

    

    “Who?”

    

    “The scientist,” Mark said. “The unarmed scientist.” He gestured to the corpse.

    

    Philip tensed up, looking away. “Yeah.”

    

    “Those weren’t our orders.”

    

    “I know,” Philip said. “It was bright, I got flustered. I just shot at whoever didn’t look like one of ours.” He looked at the corpses in the middle of the room. “But, hey. Not like it mattered anyway.”

    

    “It matters, Phil.”

    

    “I’ll get better,” Philip said, giving a smile. “I’m still new to this, hm? And hey, didn’t you say it took even you a few years to get the hang of it?” He shrugged. “We’ll say that runs in the family.”

    

    Mark pursed his lips. “Just be more careful. Be calm, always. Cover yourself. Aim. Then shoot. Got it?”

    

    “Aye aye, cap’n.” Philip resumed his inspection of the clipboard, but added: “Saw you met Oliver.”

    

    “Dault? Mhm. Your little pal.”

    

    “Don’t be too hard on the kid,” Philip said. “He’s new.”

    

“New?”



“I mean, I think so,” Philip said. “Just look at him.”



Mark looked at Dault. His armor didn’t fit, but…



“Anyways, he’s a good man,” Philip continued. “And he knows all kinds of stuff. And he’s… he looks fragile.”



    “Fragile,” Mark repeated. “God knows why he’s in this detachment at all.”

    

    “God does know,” Philip said.

    

    Mark didn’t reply. He just watched Philip continue to flip through the clipboard. Standing there, using his powerful, sleek black weapon like one would a random tree branch — he’d never looked more out of place. He looked like a puppy, wildly jumping to bypass a garden gate. 

    

    But he did lean in suddenly. “Holy shit,” he whispered. “Look.”

    

    “Phil.”

    

    “Seriously. Look.”

    

    Mark leaned in. “What am I looking at?”

    

    “Here,” Philip said. He rested the barrel of his rifle on two letters, handwritten among all the typed up text. “C.C.”

    

    “C.C.?”

    

    “Exactly.”

    

    Mark looked from the clipboard to his brother. “I’m not quite sure I get it.”

    

    “It’s her initials,” Philip said. “I can’t read most of this gibberish, but that — that says signature.”

    

    “That wasn’t her signature, though,” Mark said. “She always did a big cursive ‘C’ and wrote her last name in the loop, didn’t she?”

    

    “Doesn’t she,” Philip corrected. “But yes, you’re right. And it’s not her handwriting, either.”

    

    “So, then…” Mark said, looking at his brother to finish his sentence.

    

    “So it must be that they tried to forge her signature,” Philip concluded.

    

    “Phil, come on. You —”

    

    “Gentlemen,” came another voice.

    

    The Sergeant had joined them. He’d taken his mask off, and whereas the soldiers themselves were an odd-looking bunch beneath their armor, he wasn’t. He very much looked like an army officer — in fact, he looked so much like an army officer it was almost uncomfortable. He looked as though every part of him was contracted, clenched, impenetrable, standing by. Yet his eyes darted around the room too much. He glanced at One and Two too often. He couldn’t stay perfectly still.

    

    Mark nodded. “Sir.”

    

    The Sergeant looked right at him, then at Philip. “Is your investigation yielding fruit, Mr. Shane?”

    

    Philip looked up. “I’m sorry, sir?”

    

    “Is it yielding fruit?” he asked again. “Are you finding anything of note?”

    

    “I…” Philip looked down, pausing. “I’m not sure, sir. I can’t read the code.”

    

    “You can’t read the code.”

    

    “No, sir.”

    

    The Sergeant nodded. “Do you think you’re meant to know how to read the code?”

    

    Philip glanced at Mark. “Uh… no?”

    

    “Ah,” came the answer. “Very perceptive of you.” The Sergeant gave Mark a look, then walked off.

    

    Mark nodded his head, watching him leave.

    

    Philip gave a nervous chuckle. “That was odd.” He turned back to the clipboard he was flipping through.

    

    “Stop that,” Mark said.

    

    “Mm? Why?”

    

    “He just told you to stop that,” Mark said.

    

    Philip’s mouth twitched into a smile. He looked from Mark to the Sergeant. “That’s not what he said.”

    

    Mark sighed, grabbing Philip. “You need to get better at this, Phil.”

    

    Philip still frowned. He shrugged Mark’s hand off. “I don’t get it.”

    

    “Yeah. I know.”

