**STEP ███/███:** 

Proceed to fourth marker.

[[/div]]

[[<]]

They didn’t bother moving the bodies. Blood pooled beneath them, but none of it was in the way of One and Two, so they did nothing.



Mark watched as the Sergeant followed One and Two’s work, saying nothing and only ever approaching in tentative and uncertain ways. He followed where they went and stood never more than a few feet from them, but they never spoke to him — only to each other. They used words none of them understood, phrases that seemed nonsensical, and names that referred to nothing. Still, the Sergeant nodded along, hm-ing and huh-ing whenever a pause came up.



Eventually, though, he paused, crossing his arms and looking down at a table. At that point, Mark steeled himself and walked up to him.



“Sir,” Mark said. He stood at attention after saying that, his muscles moving almost on their own.



“Mr. Shane.” He looked him up and down. “At ease. What do you want?”



“Sergeant,” Mark said. “Permission to speak freely, sir?”



    “If it's about you and your brother’s transfers, forget it,” the Sergeant said. “You can’t bully me like you did your instructor, Mr. Shane.”

    

    “Bully you, Sergeant?”

    

    “I happen to know Golmen well. He’s been very vocal about you two. You’ve been transferred to me for a reason, soldier, and I won’t have anything like that happen here. Understood?”

    

    “Understood, sir.”

    

    “Now. Dis —”

    

    “What are we doing here, sir?” Mark said.

    

    The Sergeant paused. “Spoken a tad too freely for my taste, Shane.”

    

    “Apologies, sir,” Mark said. “I’m making a request.”

    

    “And?”

    

    “I’m requesting to know the purpose of this mission.”

    

    “You know the mission. It’s a simple escort.”

    

    “Yes, sir.”

    

    “Because it’ll be another blow to their knees,” the Sergeant said. “And they deserve it.”

    

    “Yes, sir.”

    

    “It’ll hurt them, Mr. Shane.”

    

    “Yes, sir. Is that all you know, sir?”

    

“You’re dismissed.”	



Mark nodded again, and walked away. Behind his mask he gritted his teeth. His probing had given him exactly the hint he expected: none whatsoever. Even he doesn’t have a clue. A fucking mess. Everyone. And now, he had to be in the middle of it.



    It was his fault, though. If he’d just kept a better eye on Philip, maybe he wouldn’t be here now. And if he’d known, when the sisters disappeared, that it would all spiral to this… Well. Too late. Now all he could do was try and make up for it.	

    

    “What’s he looking for?” asked someone. Oliver Dault, who had somehow soundlessly snuck up behind Mark.

    

    Mark glanced at Philip, who — with a wary glance at the Sergeant every-so-often — was still continuing his ‘investigation.’

    

    “Trouble,” Mark said. 

    

    “Lots of that here,” Dault agreed, nodding as though it were the wisest thing he’d ever heard. “He’s very dedicated.”

    

    “Yeah.”

    

    “A lot of new recruits start off looking like him. But it goes away. They all bury their noses in it. All of it. But they don’t really know what they’re looking for, they don’t know what to see, so one day they give up.”

    

    Mark sighed, standing by. His eyes were on the Sergeant, on Philip, not on Dault. 

    

    “But he knows what he’s looking for,” Dault said. “I can tell.”

    

    Mark glanced at him. 

    

    “It’s a person. He’s been talking about her.”

    

    “Of course he has,” Mark muttered under his breath.

    

    “Not very much, mind you,” Dault said. “Just that she’s very brave, very loyal. She’s missing, I think.”

    

    Hell. Mark clenched his jaw. 

    

    “I think I could help.”

    

    “Fuck off.”

    

    “I will anyway,” Dault said. “That’s why I’m asking you questions. I’m investigating.”

    

    “Uh huh,” Mark said. “And you’ve been telling people this, I assume. Telling people all about this fun little mystery.”

    

    “Nope,” Dault said.

    

    Mark raised an eyebrow. “Really.”

    

    “Why would I? They don’t have any answers. You might.”

    

From across the room came a sigh. “Alright.” The Sergeant clapped his hands, turning to his troops. “Out.”



    Back into the darkness they went, taking care not to sully their boots in blood. The corpses laid motionless across the room and watched them leave, silent.

    

    The door closed. 

    

    Twenty-five or so soldiers stood outside, with their flashlights facing the darkness of the surrounding corridors. But there had been no ambush to look out for. As always, everything went according to plan.

    

    Always according to plan.

    

    The Sergeant’s voice hummed into their comms channels. “Third on the right. Return to your positions.”

    

    Mark went to the far left of the detachment, in the back, as part of the unceremoniously named “flank team.”  Uncomfortably distanced from Philip, who stood nearest to One and Two, who walked in the center, who walked behind the Sergeant himself. Philip was on the center team. Occasionally the white-clad One and Two whispered something to him, in voices so hushed they turned to wind.

    

    Mostly, though, they continued to keep silent. They were R&D, after all. Their department didn’t share things. It didn’t talk to anyone else, if it could help it. And when bullets grazed their faces and blood pooled on the floor, they didn’t say anything either; they just watched it all. Calm.

     

    The corridors between them and their next target location were thankfully barren. They were also oddly clean — no stains on the walls, no marks, nothing. Foundation facilities were usually a hotbed of activity; they were littered with used cork-boards, pinpricks in the drywall where nails had been stuck and removed, and a general — and characteristic — lack of cleanliness. But here? Not at all. 

And now that he knew the walls were metal… he watched everything a little more carefully. The hallways were shorter, thinner, more claustrophobic than usual — and, though they’d been searching this place a while now, they hadn’t encountered any stairs, elevators, or pathways to any lower or upper levels — which was a tremendously common feature of the few sites Mark had seen. It was even more of a maze than usual, with random, unmarked turns under a terribly low, suffocating ceiling. 



From the very brief glance they had gotten of the place upon entry, it had looked small — but Mark had just assumed most of it was hidden underground, or that he simply couldn’t see the rest of it. But no. This was miniscule, undermanned, and… just different.



    Whatever this place was, it wasn’t being used like the other Foundation sites. If they even called it a ‘site.’ Its metal walls stared inwards — thin and quiet, shaking slightly as they stalked on.

