The first thing the summary announced, was the overwhelming price of enacting the contents therein. Not among the remaining pages, there were plenty of allusions to 'blood soaked warnings' and 'prolific devastation'. Perhaps their absence made the Ritual more streamlined that way. Wouldn't want a wayward mage getting the wrong idea about what exactly it is they were doing. Still, deciphering past all the code and black, blocky text had been a grim reminder of days gone by, when people had lived the events of what was now the subject of gritty, true-to-life video games. Whatever the Foundation had done to mitigate the effects of the ancient Rite had gone mostly unnoticed.



For all their posturing and back-footed attempts to collect every page of the Bible the spell was printed on, what they couldn't stop now sat behind the screen of a computer, chewing on the end of a black capped pen.



Strangely, getting ahold of the abridged work of hundreds of mages had been the //easy// part. What had been the devil in the details, was treading the fine line of 'armchair historian' and 'thaumatology expert' in online chatrooms. When most fixated enthusiasts spotted the keywords, //genetic obliteration// and //slave deterrent//, what was once hesitant curiosity tended to transition (loudly) into open ostracization. Finding an independent translator this side of the world was hard enough, but finding one with low enough self-respect to consider doing it for free launched the odds somewhere into the //galactic// realm of probability. Morality was such an anchor in this field of study, and every time Mihai nearly had a noble academic on the hook--all the swastika and red star stamped pages tended to scare them away.



Such was life.



The young mage sighed, staring at the notification tab as his most recent catch continued well into his tenth minute of typing. A single finger tapped lazily at his chin as he considered what exactly he would be lectured on this time around. Most people couldn't fathom the gravity of his work, nor the stark beauty in its execution. Doubly so, when it came time to lay on the heavy details, Mihai could not find much time to explain himself in full before the log was cut short.



//GrandOrderT// was looking to be different.



A fresh oasis in a morbid desert of neurologically deprived morons and sycophantic 'scholars' attempting to //haggle// away his leverage for their own inept studies. For every interested aspirant, there was typically a few artificial intelligences to mislead, and more than enough inbred jackasses screwing around with him to pass the time of day.



This was the only real connection he had made that hadn't ended in mockery, scorn, or an apparent threat upon his life. Refreshing as it was, Mihai had managed to soothe his choler long enough to get a genuine conversation started. Like any fish, you had to bait the waters, get a few nibbles before the big one came to bite.



Not exactly an analogy he had any experience with, but it made sense in his head. Gods willing, the mage would see soon enough.



-----



